1
00:00:53,820 --> 00:00:55,040
CONVERSATION 1
Good morning.

2
00:00:55,040 --> 00:00:56,860
My name is Bob Fetterman.

3
00:00:56,860 --> 00:00:58,420
I'm the computer technician.

4
00:00:58,420 --> 00:01:00,940
Didn't I call for you last week?

5
00:01:00,940 --> 00:01:01,800
Maybe.

6
00:01:01,800 --> 00:01:03,620
I work for Alpha Maintenance.

7
00:01:03,620 --> 00:01:09,520
I imagine someone from your office called our central office, and I'm the engineer designated to your case.

8
00:01:09,520 --> 00:01:14,420
Yeah, I spoke to a lady there last Tuesday, which is now ten days ago.

9
00:01:14,420 --> 00:01:20,700
I made it clear it was an urgent matter, and please send someone as soon as possible.

10
00:01:20,700 --> 00:01:23,540
Couldn't you or someone else have come sooner?

11
00:01:23,540 --> 00:01:27,180
I'm sorry, but I'm not in charge of scheduling all logistics.

12
00:01:27,180 --> 00:01:28,580
I simply do not know.

13
00:01:28,580 --> 00:01:32,980
All I can tell you is I got the call this week and came as soon as I could.

14
00:01:32,980 --> 00:01:34,140
I see.

15
00:01:34,140 --> 00:01:39,460
Well, I'll have you know, my manager is rather indignant that it's taken you this long to come.

16
00:01:39,460 --> 00:01:45,460
I understand it might not be your fault, but nevertheless, you should know what the feeling is around here.

17
00:01:45,460 --> 00:01:48,980
I understand, and I'm sorry for any inconvenience.

18
00:01:48,980 --> 00:01:53,560
I will try to resolve your computer problem as swiftly and optimally as I can.

19
00:01:53,560 --> 00:01:55,620
What and where is the issue?

20
00:01:55,620 --> 00:01:57,140
It's this machine right here.

21
00:01:57,140 --> 00:02:02,120
It was running slow for a while, I guess, and gradually got worse and worse.

22
00:02:02,120 --> 00:02:05,200
Last Tuesday, it just stopped working altogether.

23
00:02:05,200 --> 00:02:09,260
If you switch it on, you will see what I mean.

24
00:02:09,260 --> 00:02:10,640
OK, do you know if it's backed up?

25
00:02:10,640 --> 00:02:12,160
Backed up?

26
00:02:12,160 --> 00:02:16,440
I mean, do you know if its contents have been copied and saved somewhere else?

27
00:02:16,440 --> 00:02:18,000
I don't know.

28
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:32,580
Well, depending on the ensuing operative scenario, if I need to wipe the system directories clean in order to reconfigure default protocols, then my first intervention should be to intricately retrieve all inventories that I can.

29
00:02:32,580 --> 00:02:37,320
Otherwise, all data that was stored on its hard drive will be forever lost.

30
00:02:37,320 --> 00:02:38,880
My manager would know.

31
00:02:38,880 --> 00:02:47,380
Let me go get him.

32
00:02:47,380 --> 00:03:04,800
Q1. What did the woman do last Tuesday?

33
00:03:04,800 --> 00:03:22,460
Q2. What does the man say he did when he got the call?

34
00:03:22,460 --> 00:03:41,080
Q3. What is the feeling about the problem around the woman's workplace?

35
00:03:41,080 --> 00:03:57,940
Q4. What does the woman say her manager would know?

36
00:03:57,940 --> 00:04:01,120
CONVERSATION 2
Have you given more thought to studying law?

37
00:04:01,120 --> 00:04:03,340
I've been thinking about it a lot.

38
00:04:03,340 --> 00:04:03,840
Yes.

39
00:04:03,840 --> 00:04:06,980
In fact, this is why I wished to speak to you.

40
00:04:06,980 --> 00:04:09,560
I wanted to hear more about what it's like.

41
00:04:09,560 --> 00:04:13,300
A career in law is a beautiful thing, in my opinion.

42
00:04:13,300 --> 00:04:17,300
Many people will say it's a boring field, but I disagree.

43
00:04:17,300 --> 00:04:22,020
The thing is, there are many different branches of law and many different jobs.

44
00:04:22,020 --> 00:04:25,500
You are involved with buildings and construction, right?

45
00:04:25,500 --> 00:04:26,500
Correct.

46
00:04:26,500 --> 00:04:29,580
I'm a solicitor specializing in real estate.

47
00:04:29,580 --> 00:04:32,700
Most of my work involves mediating land disputes.

48
00:04:32,700 --> 00:04:34,960
Do you mind giving me an example?

49
00:04:34,960 --> 00:04:36,160
Sure.

50
00:04:36,160 --> 00:04:41,540
So in many cases, I am employed to arbitrate between two clashing parties.

51
00:04:41,540 --> 00:04:49,780
Let's say, for example, there are two landowners who are in a thorny disagreement about something like the borders of their adjacent plots.

52
00:04:49,780 --> 00:04:54,160
Someone may employ me to break the deadlock and resolve the dispute.

53
00:04:54,160 --> 00:04:58,500
Often, my work involves negotiating agreements, plain and simple.

54
00:04:58,500 --> 00:05:08,900
So you are saying that a lot of what you do is fix people's legal issues, like you try to find a solution that benefits everyone and that prevents a problem from escalating.

55
00:05:08,900 --> 00:05:10,680
Exactly.

56
00:05:10,680 --> 00:05:11,760
Well put.

57
00:05:11,760 --> 00:05:18,660
You need to remember that courts, like all bureaucracies, really can be very messy, frustrating and expensive.

58
00:05:18,660 --> 00:05:24,060
For this reason, it's usually better to resolve problems outside of court whenever possible.

59
00:05:24,060 --> 00:05:25,040
I see.

60
00:05:25,040 --> 00:05:29,720
So besides knowing the law inside out, you need good people skills, right?

61
00:05:29,720 --> 00:05:40,140
Of course, any job that involves people requires good interpersonal skills, and any law professional needs to be very eloquent at articulating their argument.

62
00:05:40,140 --> 00:05:40,600
Right.

63
00:05:40,600 --> 00:05:43,580
So I've been thinking about studying criminal law.

64
00:05:43,580 --> 00:05:45,980
I think it sounds very interesting.

65
00:05:45,980 --> 00:05:47,320
Hmm, yes.

66
00:05:47,320 --> 00:05:52,100
I'd say there's more money in property law, but the stakes are higher in criminal law.

67
00:05:52,100 --> 00:06:01,420
I can see how you would find it more interesting.

68
00:06:01,420 --> 00:06:19,300
Q5. What will many people say about a career in law?

69
00:06:19,300 --> 00:06:36,920
Q6. What does the man say most of his work involves?

70
00:06:36,920 --> 00:06:57,320
Q7. Why does the man say it's usually better to resolve problems outside of court whenever possible?

71
00:06:57,320 --> 00:07:45,200
Q8. What does the man think is required of any law professional?

72
00:07:45,200 --> 00:07:53,460
PASSAGE 1
When a person tries to access a memory, their brains quickly examine everything stored in it to find the relevant information.

73
00:07:53,460 --> 00:07:56,800
But as we age, many of us have difficulty retrieving memories.

74
00:07:56,800 --> 00:08:19,180
In a review published in the journal _Trends in Cognitive Sciences_ on February 11, researchers propose an explanation for why this might be happening: the brains of older adults allocate more space to accumulated knowledge and have more material to navigate when attempting to access memories.

75
00:08:19,180 --> 00:08:32,340
While this wealth of prior knowledge can make memory retrieval challenging, the researchers say it has its upsides—this life experience can aid with creativity and decision-making.

76
00:08:32,340 --> 00:08:53,860
Researchers Tarek Amer, Jordana Wynn and Lynn Hasher looked at several behavioral and brain-imaging studies, which show that older adults have difficulty suppressing information that is no longer relevant and that when searching for a specific memory, they often retrieve other, irrelevant memories along with it.

77
00:08:53,860 --> 00:09:04,140
The studies also showed that when given a cognitive task, older adults rely more heavily on previous knowledge than younger adults do.

78
00:09:04,140 --> 00:09:17,880
While the researchers focus primarily on the difficulties that these disorderly-crowded memories may pose, they also highlight a few situations in which these same memory patterns may be useful.

79
00:09:17,880 --> 00:09:28,960
Evidence suggests that older adults show preserved, and at times enhanced, creativity as a function of enriched memories, the researchers write.

80
00:09:28,960 --> 00:09:46,980
They further hypothesize that older adults may be well served by their prior knowledge when it comes to decision-making, where they can pull on their accumulated wisdom.

81
00:09:46,980 --> 00:10:07,000
Q9. What did the researchers do in their review in the journal _Trends in Cognitive Sciences_?

82
00:10:07,000 --> 00:10:25,700
Q10. What do several behavioral and brain-imaging studies show?

83
00:10:25,700 --> 00:10:43,500
Q11. What does evidence suggest regarding older adults?

84
00:10:43,500 --> 00:10:48,700
PASSAGE 2
There are at least four major problems with work in America today.

85
00:10:48,700 --> 00:10:52,720
First, work can be alienating.

86
00:10:52,720 --> 00:11:03,120
Workers are often not in control of how they work, when they work, what is done with the goods and services they produce, and what is done with the profits made from their work.

87
00:11:03,120 --> 00:11:07,680
Second, workers are not paid the full value of their labor.

88
00:11:07,680 --> 00:11:16,580
Real wages have not kept pace with productivity, driving economic inequality and a decline in labor's share of income.

89
00:11:16,580 --> 00:11:19,420
Third, people are time poor.

90
00:11:19,420 --> 00:11:23,500
In the U.S., full-time employees work an average of 8.

91
00:11:23,500 --> 00:11:28,840
72 hours per day despite productivity increases.

92
00:11:28,840 --> 00:11:38,160
Long working hours contribute to the feeling of time poverty, which has an adverse effect on psychological well-being.

93
00:11:38,160 --> 00:11:42,860
Fourth, automation puts jobs and wages at risk.

94
00:11:42,860 --> 00:11:58,080
Technological innovation could in theory liberate people from the 40-hour workweek, but unless changes are made to the structure of work, automation will simply exert downward pressure on wages and further diminish work opportunities.

95
00:11:58,080 --> 00:12:02,380
So, what can be done to alleviate these problems?

96
00:12:02,380 --> 00:12:07,360
There are competing visions of the best way to arrive at a solution.

97
00:12:07,360 --> 00:12:11,280
One vision is what's known as the post-work position.

98
00:12:11,280 --> 00:12:19,400
The post-work theorists believe that work should not hold a central position in our lives or take up so much of our time.

99
00:12:19,400 --> 00:12:31,620
They assert that, although doing some work might be necessary, meaning, purpose and social value reside in the communities and relationships built and sustained outside of the workplace.

100
00:12:31,620 --> 00:12:42,720
Thus, life should be seen as an end in itself.

101
00:12:42,720 --> 00:13:00,960
Q12. What do we learn about American workers from the passage?

102
00:13:00,960 --> 00:13:19,860
Q13. What does the passage say long working hours contribute to?

103
00:13:19,860 --> 00:13:37,440
Q14. What does automation do according to the passage?

104
00:13:37,440 --> 00:14:21,920
Q15. What do post-work theorists advocate?

105
00:14:21,920 --> 00:14:52,420
RECORDING 1
Recent studies show that self-control and persistence are important predictors of academic success.

106
00:14:52,420 --> 00:14:57,647
But how do children learn these skills?

107
00:14:57,647 --> 00:15:05,862
Previous research has shown that children can learn persistence from adults.

108
00:15:05,862 --> 00:15:23,785
For example, if children see an adult work hard to achieve a goal, they are more likely to persist in their own difficult tasks.

109
00:15:23,785 --> 00:15:36,480
But what if children only see the end result of an adult's hard work, not the process?

110
00:15:36,480 --> 00:15:43,948
To explore this question, researchers studied 15-month-old babies.

111
00:15:43,948 --> 00:15:57,391
In one experiment, the babies watched an adult struggle to open a container for 30 seconds before succeeding.

112
00:15:57,391 --> 00:16:09,340
In another experiment, the babies watched the adult open the container easily on the first try.

113
00:16:09,340 --> 00:16:12,100
Then, the babies were given a toy that played music when they pressed a hidden button.

114
00:16:12,100 --> 00:16:40,941
The researchers found that the babies who had watched the adult struggle pressed the button more times and for longer periods of time than the babies who had watched the adult succeed easily.

115
00:16:40,941 --> 00:16:51,429
The results suggest that babies can learn persistence from watching adults struggle.

116
00:16:51,429 --> 00:17:04,538
This is important because it shows that children don't need to be explicitly taught persistence.

117
00:17:04,538 --> 00:17:15,900
They can learn it simply by observing the behavior of adults around them.

118
00:17:15,900 --> 00:17:29,563
The researchers also found that the babies were more likely to persist if the adult made eye contact with them and talked to them while struggling.

119
00:17:29,563 --> 00:17:35,870
This suggests that social interaction plays an important role in learning persistence.

120
00:17:35,870 --> 00:17:46,380
Q16. What are important predictors of academic success according to recent studies?

121
00:17:46,380 --> 00:18:08,660
Q17. How did the babies in the experiment activate the music-playing toy?

122
00:18:08,660 --> 00:18:26,060
Q18. What can babies learn from watching adults struggle according to the study?

123
00:18:26,060 --> 00:19:13,880
RECORDING 2
If chronic lateness is a problem for you, you're not alone.

124
00:19:13,880 --> 00:19:49,945
Many people struggle with being on time, and it can have negative consequences for their careers, relationships and self-esteem.

125
00:19:49,945 --> 00:20:09,780
But there are things you can do to overcome habitual lateness.

126
00:20:09,780 --> 00:20:17,880
First, if mornings are a struggle for you, do as much as you can to ready yourself the night before.

127
00:20:17,880 --> 00:20:19,708
Lay out your clothes, pack your bag, and prepare your lunch.

128
00:20:19,708 --> 00:20:22,200
This will save you time in the morning and reduce the stress of rushing around.

129
00:20:22,200 --> 00:20:27,457
Second, learn to leave time in between tasks and meetings.

130
00:20:27,457 --> 00:20:33,240
It's easy to overschedule yourself, but this often leads to lateness.

131
00:20:33,240 --> 00:20:52,780
If you have back-to-back meetings, you won't have time to get from one place to another, and you'll end up being late.

132
00:20:52,780 --> 00:20:55,174
Third, calculate transit time between activities and then add 10 to 30 minutes for unexpected delays.

133
00:20:55,174 --> 00:20:58,316
Traffic, public transportation problems, and other unforeseen events can all make you late, so it's important to build in a buffer.

134
00:20:58,316 --> 00:20:59,662
Finally, be honest with yourself about why you're late.

135
00:20:59,662 --> 00:21:00,710
Are you trying to do too much?

136
00:21:00,710 --> 00:21:01,757
Do you underestimate how long tasks take?

137
00:21:01,757 --> 00:21:04,151
Once you identify the root cause of your lateness, you can take steps to address it.

138
00:21:04,151 --> 00:21:06,844
Remember, being on time is a habit, and like any habit, it takes time and effort to change.

139
00:21:06,844 --> 00:21:08,340
But with practice, you can become a more punctual person.

140
00:21:08,340 --> 00:21:25,980
Q19. What is the speaker's purpose of giving this talk?

141
00:21:25,980 --> 00:21:44,120
Q20. What should you do if mornings are a struggle for you?

142
00:21:44,120 --> 00:22:03,420
Q21. According to the speaker, what can we do to combat unexpected delays?

143
00:22:03,420 --> 00:22:07,780
RECORDING 3
Do you think Bostonians put out the welcome mat?

144
00:22:07,780 --> 00:22:11,340
Do you find other cities to be more or less polite?

145
00:22:11,340 --> 00:22:38,247
Different cities have different feels, and one of the things that contributes to that feel is the level of politeness of the people who live there.

146
00:22:38,247 --> 00:22:58,945
Some cities are known for being friendly and welcoming, while others are known for being more reserved or even rude.

147
00:22:58,945 --> 00:23:08,259
Boston, for example, is often criticized for being unfriendly.

148
00:23:08,259 --> 00:23:25,853
Many people say that Bostonians are not as willing to help strangers as people in other cities.

149
00:23:25,853 --> 00:23:42,411
They also say that Bostonians are more likely to be rude or impatient in public places.

150
00:23:42,411 --> 00:23:47,585
But is this really true?

151
00:23:47,585 --> 00:24:05,179
Some people argue that Bostonians are just more direct and less likely to engage in small talk.

152
00:24:05,179 --> 00:24:25,877
They say that this doesn't mean they're unfriendly; it just means they have a different way of interacting with people.

153
00:24:25,877 --> 00:24:41,400
Other cities, like Paris and New York City, are also known for being less polite.

154
00:24:41,400 --> 00:24:43,300
But these cities are also major tourist destinations, and the large number of tourists can make it difficult for locals to be friendly all the time.

155
00:24:43,300 --> 00:24:44,397
In contrast, cities like Nashville and Austin are known for being very friendly and welcoming.

156
00:24:44,397 --> 00:24:45,932
People in these cities are more likely to smile at strangers, hold doors open for them, and offer help when needed.

157
00:24:45,932 --> 00:24:46,589
So, what can cities do to become more polite?

158
00:24:46,589 --> 00:24:48,271
Some experts say that cities can follow the examples set by Paris and New York City, which have launched campaigns to encourage politeness.

159
00:24:48,271 --> 00:24:49,221
These campaigns include posters, advertisements, and public events that promote kindness and respect.

160
00:24:49,221 --> 00:24:50,902
Other experts say that cities should focus on improving public spaces, such as parks and sidewalks, to make them more pleasant and inviting.

161
00:24:50,902 --> 00:24:52,071
When people feel good about their surroundings, they are more likely to be polite to others.

162
00:24:52,071 --> 00:24:53,460
Q22. What is said about different cities according to the speaker?

163
00:24:53,460 --> 00:25:26,110
Q23. What do many people say about Bostonians?

164
00:25:26,110 --> 00:25:26,160
Q24. Why does the speaker say Paris and New York City are less polite?

165
00:25:26,160 --> 00:26:11,004
Q25. What can cities do to become more polite according to some experts?
